- `action(n1, "start the process")`: Hallucinated action; the source text describes starting by making an appointment, not performing a distinct "start" action (SMC1).
- `action(n11, "You have completed this procedure")`: Hallucinated action not present in the source text (SMC1).
- `action(n4, "do you need your hair washed")`, `action(n6, "do you want your hair dyed")`, `action(n8, "do you need a haircut")`: The source describes these as conditions ("If you need/want..."), not as explicit process actions; they should be modeled via branch evaluations (SMC2).
- `defines(v1, ...)`, `defines(v2, ...)`, `defines(v3, ...)`: The variable ranges `{}` are empty instead of specifying valid options like true/false or yes/no (SMC6).
- `branch(b1, XOR)`, `branch(b2, XOR)`, `branch(b3, XOR)`: Missing `guard` statements on their outgoing edges to evaluate the conditions described in the text (SMC4).
- `precedence(n5, c1)` and `precedence(n7, c1)`: Taking an earlier optional action (washing or dyeing) incorrectly routes the flow directly to `c1` and "pay the hairdresser", bypassing the subsequent optional steps (dyeing, cutting). The text describes these as independent sequential choices, not mutually exclusive ones (SMC4).

Structural Check
- [S1] STC11 failed: Each variable definition must have a valid range with at least two distinct values. | Variable `v1` has an invalid range `[]`. | Variable `v2` has an invalid range `[]`. | Variable `v3` has an invalid range `[]`.
- [S2] STC16 failed: Every outgoing edge of XOR/OR branches must have exactly one guard. | XOR/OR edge `b1 -> n5` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b1 -> n6` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b2 -> n7` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b2 -> n8` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b3 -> n9` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b3 -> c1` has 0 guards; exactly one is required.